2. Findings
Finding 1: SQL Injection (Search / Filter Functionality)
Risk Rating: High
Description: The product search and order-filter endpoints accept user-supplied input that is concatenated directly into SQL
queries. An attacker can inject malicious SQL to extract, modify, or delete data from the underlying database.
Sample Evidence / Output:
Request: GET /api/search?q=pizza' OR '1'='1
Response excerpt: {"results":[{"order_id":1001,"user":"userA@example.com",...},
{"order_id":1002,"user":"userB@example.com",...}]} (unexpected full dataset returned)
Potential Impact: Full or partial database compromise, exposure of customer PII, order history, and possible authentication
bypass.
Finding 2: Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) – Order Details
Risk Rating: High
Description: The order-details API endpoint uses a sequential or predictable order identifier in the URL (e.g.,
/api/orders/10482). The application does not adequately verify that the authenticated user is authorized to view the
requested order. By changing the ID, a user can access other customers’ order details.
Sample Evidence / Output:
Authenticated as User A → Request: GET /api/orders/10483
Response: {"order_id":10483,"customer":"User B","address":"...","items":[...],"payment_last4":"4242"}
Potential Impact: Unauthorized disclosure of other customers’ personal data, delivery addresses, and partial payment
information; violation of privacy expectations and possible regulatory issues.
3. Recommendations
For SQL Injection:
• Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) or a trusted ORM for all database access.
• Never concatenate user input directly into SQL strings.
• Apply strict input validation and least-privilege database accounts as additional layers of defense.
For Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR):
• Enforce authorization checks on every request that accesses an object (order, profile, etc.).
• Verify that the currently authenticated user owns or has explicit permission to view the requested resource.
• Prefer indirect references (e.g., random tokens or mapped IDs) instead of sequential database primary keys where
practical.
General: Prioritize remediation of both High findings. Re-test after fixes are applied. Consider incorporating automated
SAST/DAST and secure code review into the development pipeline.
